duplicate ids 0
{"id": "M4-c067", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the standard deviation of X?", "back": "sigma_X = sqrt(Var(X)), the nonnegative square root of the variance (Lesson 2, Definition 2.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c068", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the correlation of X and Y?", "back": "rho(X,Y) = Cov(X,Y)/(sigma_X sigma_Y), defined when both standard deviations are positive. A constant has no correlation (Definition 1.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c069", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is covariance symmetric in its two arguments?", "back": "Yes. Hypothesis: finitely many values. Cov(X,Y) = Cov(Y,X) (Proposition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c070", "lesson": "L05", "front": "How does covariance scale?", "back": "Hypothesis: finitely many values. Cov(aX + b, cY + d) = ac Cov(X,Y) for real a, b, c and d (Proposition 1.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c071", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What bound does Cauchy-Schwarz give for covariance and correlation?", "back": "Hypothesis: finitely many values. Cov(X,Y)^2 <= Var(X) Var(Y). So when both standard deviations are positive, -1 <= rho <= 1 (Proposition 1.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c072", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is correlation unchanged by positive affine maps?", "back": "Yes, when sigma_X > 0 and sigma_Y > 0. For a, c > 0 and any b and d, rho(aX + b, cY + d) = rho(X,Y) (Proposition 1.6).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c073", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the correlation of X and Y = aX + b when a > 0 and sigma_X > 0?", "back": "1 (Example 1.7): Cov(X,Y) = a Var(X) and sigma_Y = a sigma_X.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c074", "lesson": "L05", "front": "For X uniform on {-1, 0, 1} and Y = X^2, what is Var(Y)?", "back": "2/9, since E[Y] = 2/3 and E[Y^2] = E[X^4] = 2/3, so Var(Y) = 2/3 - 4/9 (Remark 1.8).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c075", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the covariance matrix of a vector X of random variables X_1 to X_n?", "back": "The n by n matrix with entries Sigma_ij = Cov(X_i, X_j). Its diagonal entries are the variances (Definition 2.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c076", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What does it mean for a real symmetric matrix M to be positive semidefinite?", "back": "w^T M w >= 0 for every vector w (Definition 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c077", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is every covariance matrix positive semidefinite?", "back": "Yes. A covariance matrix is symmetric, and w^T Sigma w = Var(sum of w_i X_i) >= 0 for every w (Proposition 2.3). The hypothesis is finitely many values.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c078", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What bounds hold for the entries of a covariance matrix?", "back": "Hypothesis: X has finitely many values. Every diagonal entry is nonnegative, and Sigma_ij^2 <= Sigma_ii Sigma_jj for all i and j (Proposition 2.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c079", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Does zero correlation mean the two variables are unrelated?", "back": "No. Remark 1.8 gives Y = X^2 with X uniform on {-1, 0, 1}: the correlation is 0 while Y is a function of X. Correlation measures linear association.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c080", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is covariance unit-free, like correlation?", "back": "No. Cov(100X, 100Y) = 10^4 Cov(X,Y) (Proposition 1.2), while the correlation is unchanged by the same scaling when both standard deviations are positive (Proposition 1.6).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c081", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is any symmetric matrix with positive diagonal entries a covariance matrix?", "back": "No. The matrix [[1, 2], [2, 1]] is symmetric with positive diagonal, but for a vector with finitely many values Proposition 2.4 needs 4 <= 1, which is false.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c082", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1 Part A, what is the largest entry error of the sample covariance matrix at n = 20000, against Sigma?", "back": "0.00002223, from the seeded sample at n = 20000.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M4-c083", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1 Part B, what sample correlation does the seeded run give at n = 20000, against rho = 0.6?", "back": "0.592936, an error of -0.007064.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part B (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M4-c084", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the normal distribution N(mu, sigma^2)?", "back": "The law with density exp(-(x - mu)^2/(2 sigma^2))/(sigma sqrt(2 pi)) on the real line, for sigma > 0 (Definition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c221", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the standard normal law?", "back": "N(0,1), with density phi(z) = e^(-z^2/2)/sqrt(2 pi). A variable with this law is a standard normal variable (Definition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c085", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the value of the Gaussian integral?", "back": "The integral of e^(-x^2/2) over the real line equals sqrt(2 pi) (Proposition 1.2). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c086", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Is the normal density a probability density?", "back": "Yes. For sigma > 0 and real mu, the density is nonnegative and integrates to 1 (Proposition 1.3). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c087", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What does standardization give for a normal variable?", "back": "If X ~ N(mu, sigma^2) with sigma > 0, then (X - mu)/sigma has the law N(0,1). Conversely, if Z is standard normal, then mu + sigma Z has the law N(mu, sigma^2) (Proposition 1.4). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c088", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is E[Z^2] for a standard normal variable Z?", "back": "1, so Var(Z) = 1 (Proposition 1.5, by integration by parts). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c089", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is a chi-square variable with nu degrees of freedom?", "back": "W_nu = the sum of Z_i^2 for i = 1 to nu, for independent standard normal Z_i, with nu a positive integer (Definition 2.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c090", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What Laplace-transform identity does Lemma 2.3 give for a squared normal?", "back": "For s >= 0, E[e^(-s Z^2)] = (1 + 2s)^(-1/2) for a standard normal Z. Under Assumption 1 (Lemma 2.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c091", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is E[1/W] for a chi-square variable W with nu degrees of freedom?", "back": "Hypothesis: nu a positive integer. If nu > 2, E[1/W] = 1/(nu - 2). If nu <= 2, E[1/W] is infinite (Proposition 2.4). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c092", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the Student-t distribution with nu degrees of freedom?", "back": "The law of t = Z / sqrt(W/nu), with Z standard normal and W chi-square with nu degrees of freedom, independent of Z. Only positive integer nu is covered (Definition 2.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c093", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is the mean of the Student-t with nu > 2 degrees of freedom?", "back": "0 (Proposition 2.6). Hypothesis: nu a positive integer. Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c094", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is Var(t_5)?", "back": "5/3, from nu/(nu - 2) with nu = 5 (Proposition 2.6). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c095", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Does a Student-t variable have variance 1 because it is standardized?", "back": "No. A Student-t with nu > 2 degrees of freedom has mean 0 and variance nu/(nu - 2), not 1 (Proposition 2.6). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c096", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Does every Student-t have a finite variance, as every normal does?", "back": "No. For nu <= 2, E[t^2] = nu E[1/W] is infinite (Proposition 2.6). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c097", "lesson": "L06", "front": "Can the variance of N(mu, sigma^2) be set separately from its mean?", "back": "No. Both parameters are determined by the law: E[X] = mu and Var(X) = sigma^2 (Proposition 1.5). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c098", "lesson": "L06", "front": "In Lab 1 Part A, what is the sample variance of t at nu = 5, against 5/3?", "back": "1.669114 against the formula 1.666667. The relative error 0.001469 uses unrounded values (n = 100000 seeded draws).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED), seed 20260602 + nu"}
{"id": "M4-c099", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the moment-generating function of X, and what is its domain?", "back": "M_X(t) = E[e^(tX)] for each real t where this expectation is finite. The domain is the set of such t and always contains 0, where M_X(0) = 1 (Definition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c100", "lesson": "L07", "front": "Is the moment-generating function of a finitely valued X finite for every real t?", "back": "Yes. Its domain is the whole real line (Proposition 1.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c101", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the moment-generating function of N(mu, sigma^2)?", "back": "exp(mu t + sigma^2 t^2/2) for every real t, for sigma > 0 (Proposition 1.3). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c102", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the moment-generating function of the square of a standard normal?", "back": "E[e^(tZ^2)] = (1 - 2t)^(-1/2) for t < 1/2. The domain is (-infinity, 1/2) (Proposition 1.4). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c103", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the complex exponential e^(ia)?", "back": "cos a + i sin a, with modulus 1 (Definition 2.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c104", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the characteristic function of X?", "back": "phi_X(t) = E[e^(itX)] for each real t (Definition 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c105", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What bounds the characteristic function of X?", "back": "Hypothesis: X has finitely many values or has a density (Assumption 1(c) in the density case). Then |phi_X(t)| <= 1 for every real t, and phi_X(0) = 1 (Proposition 2.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c106", "lesson": "L07", "front": "How does the characteristic function transform under Y = aX + b?", "back": "phi_(aX+b)(t) = e^(itb) phi_X(at) for every real t (Proposition 2.4).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c107", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the characteristic function of a two-point law with P(X = a) = p and P(X = b) = 1 - p, for 0 < p < 1?", "back": "phi_X(t) = p e^(ita) + (1 - p) e^(itb) (Proposition 2.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c230", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is |phi_X(t)|^2 for a two-point law with P(X = a) = p and P(X = b) = 1 - p, for 0 < p < 1?", "back": "p^2 + (1 - p)^2 + 2p(1 - p) cos(t(a - b)) (Proposition 2.5).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c108", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the moment-generating function of a sum of independent finitely valued variables?", "back": "Hypothesis: X and Y independent with finitely many values. M_(X+Y)(t) = M_X(t) M_Y(t) for every real t (Problem P3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c109", "lesson": "L07", "front": "Is the moment-generating function of every random variable finite for every real t?", "back": "No. For W = Z^2, E[e^(tW)] is infinite for t >= 1/2 (Proposition 1.4). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c110", "lesson": "L07", "front": "Is the moment-generating function of a sum the sum of the two functions?", "back": "No. If X = Y, then M_(X+Y)(t) = M_X(2t), which is 1 at t = 0, while M_X(0) + M_Y(0) = 2.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c111", "lesson": "L07", "front": "Does the characteristic function exist only for a variable with a density?", "back": "No. It exists for every finitely valued variable (Proposition 2.3), and the two-point law of Proposition 2.5 has no density.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c112", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the largest absolute difference between Simpson's rule and the closed-form normal MGF in Lab 1 Part A?", "back": "7.11e-13, at t = 2 (closed form 244.69193226), for X ~ N(0.5, 1.5^2). This is a numerical quadrature result, not a simulation.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (numerical quadrature)"}
{"id": "M4-c113", "lesson": "L07", "front": "What is the seeded sample mean of cos(X) at t = 1 in Lab 1 Part C, against the real part of phi(1)?", "back": "-0.062124, against -0.062110 for the real part of phi(1).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part C (SIMULATED), seed 20260701"}
{"id": "M4-c174", "lesson": "L06", "front": "When are variables X and Y independent in Remark 2.2?", "back": "When P(X in A, Y in B) = P(X in A) P(Y in B) for all sets A and B (Remark 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c191", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What covariance matrix does the two-asset construction X1 = S1 Z1, X2 = S2(rho Z1 + sqrt(1 - rho^2) Z2) have?", "back": "[[S1^2, rho S1 S2], [rho S1 S2, S2^2]]. Under the hypotheses of Example 2.5 (S1, S2 > 0; |rho| < 1; Z1, Z2 independent with mean 0 and variance 1), and assuming its Assumption 1, that the rules of Proposition 1.1 hold for Z1 and Z2 (not proved here).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c205", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What is E[Z] for a standard normal variable Z?", "back": "0, because z phi(z) is odd (Proposition 1.5). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c210", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is covariance additive in its first argument?", "back": "Yes. Hypothesis: finitely many values. Cov(X + Z, Y) = Cov(X,Y) + Cov(Z,Y) (Proposition 1.1).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c216", "lesson": "L06", "front": "What integral gives E[1/W] for a chi-square variable W with nu degrees of freedom?", "back": "E[1/W] = the integral from 0 to infinity of (1 + 2s)^(-nu/2) ds, a value in (0, infinity] (Lemma 2.3). Under Assumption 1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M4-c241", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1 Part A, what is the largest entry error of the sample covariance matrix at n = 100, against Sigma?", "back": "0.00016257, in the (2,2) entry of the sample matrix (one run).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, Part A (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "M4-c242", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is the covariance of X with itself nonnegative?", "back": "Yes. Cov(X,X) = Var(X) >= 0, because Var(X) is a finite sum of squares times probabilities. Hypothesis: finitely many values. (Proposition 1.1(iv)).", "grade": "T"}
